<BaseLayout title="LU7 (Lieque) — Broken Sequence Point" description="Learn how to locate and stimulate LU7 (Lieque) — the Broken Sequence point. Benefits include headache and stiff neck relief, cough support, and sinus congestion relief." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points" }, { label: "LU7 (Lieque)", href: "/points/lu7-lieque-acupressure-point" }]} datePublished="2026-08-19" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">LU7 (Lieque) — Broken Sequence</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">LU7, known as Lieque or Broken Sequence, is a Lung meridian point traditionally used for the classic "headache with stiff neck" pattern — frontal pain that spreads toward the back of the neck — along with coughs, sore throat, and nasal congestion.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Lung (LU)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Thumb side of the forearm, just above the wrist</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Frontal headache with a stiff neck, cough, sore throat, nasal congestion</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Easy to reach yourself — the crossed-hands trick finds it in seconds</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">30–60 seconds per side</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–3 times daily</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4 (Hegu)</a>, <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">GB20 (Fengchi)</a>, <a href="/points/cv22-tiantu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">CV22 (Tiantu)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Recent wrist injury, sprain, or a fracture in the thumb-side (radial) area (see Precautions)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why LU7 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">LU7 is the Luo (connecting) point of the Lung meridian and one of the eight confluent points, opening to the Conception (Ren) meridian. Its position on the thumb side of the forearm, just above the wrist, makes it one of the easiest upper-limb points to reach for self-care — you can press it at a desk without removing a jacket or changing position. Because the Lung meridian is classically linked to the head, throat, and upper body, LU7 is a common first choice for headache that begins at the forehead and radiates toward the stiff, tense neck that accompanies it.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">LU7 belongs to the same band of arm and wrist points used throughout the site for head and neck complaints, and it is a frequently recommended point for the early stages of a head cold. Applied gently while turning the head, it is also a practical point for people who hold tension in the neck after desk work, screen time, or long commutes. In traditional needle practice LU7 is classically paired with KI6 (Zhaohai), and in self-acupressure the same pairing is used as one of the classic point combinations for throat and neck complaints.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/lu7-lieque-location.webp" alt="LU7 acupressure point location just above the wrist on the thumb side" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">LU7 sits on the radial (thumb) side of the forearm, 1.5 cun above the wrist crease — about the width of two fingers — in the small depression just above the radial styloid process, the bony bump at the outer edge of the wrist. The simplest way to find it: cross the thumb and index finger of both hands, like a handshake made with the forearms crossed, and the tip of your index finger will land on or very near the LU7 point.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) it is the hollow on the thumb side of the forearm, not the center of the wrist; (2) pressing toward the bone produces a deep, dull ache that may travel toward the thumb; (3) the depression sits about two finger widths above the wrist crease, and the crossed-hands trick lands your index fingertip on it.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use LU7 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 dark:border-emerald-900/40 dark:bg-emerald-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You have a frontal headache that spreads into a tight, stiff neck</li>
          <li>• Your throat feels scratchy or a cough is building — the first signs of a cold</li>
          <li>• Your nose is congested and you feel stuffed up</li>
          <li>• Your neck is tense after desk work, screen time, or a long commute</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You recently sprained, fractured, or had surgery on the wrist or forearm</li>
          <li>• There is a cast, swelling, bruising, or broken skin over the point</li>
          <li>• You have numbness or tingling in the thumb or hand</li>
          <li>• The headache is sudden, severe, or comes with fever, vision changes, or vomiting</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: LU7 is a comfort practice for head-and-neck tension and cold symptoms, not first aid for an acute wrist injury or a sudden severe headache.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Headache with neck stiffness:</strong> LU7 is the traditional point for frontal headache that radiates into a tense neck — the classic "headache connected to the neck" pattern.</li>
      <li><strong>Cough:</strong> It is used for a dry, irritating cough and for tightness in the chest during the early stages of a cold.</li>
      <li><strong>Sore throat:</strong> LU7 helps soothe throat irritation and hoarseness, especially when combined with warmth and fluids.</li>
      <li><strong>Nasal congestion:</strong> It supports the upper respiratory defenses and is commonly pressed for stuffy or running noses.</li>
      <li><strong>Stiff neck:</strong> Pressed gently while rotating the head, LU7 helps loosen a stiff neck after poor sleep or long periods at a desk.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Massage LU7</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Lay the forearm palm-down on a support, press with the opposite thumb toward the bone, and gently rotate the head and neck while holding — this combination is what makes LU7 useful for the headache-with-stiff-neck pattern.</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Rest your forearm on a flat surface with your palm facing down to relax the wrist.</li>
      <li>Place the pad of your opposite thumb in the depression just above the wrist, on the thumb side, against the radial styloid process.</li>
      <li>Press firmly toward the bone until you feel a deep, dull ache that stops short of sharp pain.</li>
      <li>Hold for 30–60 seconds while slowly turning your head from side to side and nodding gently.</li>
      <li>Repeat on both arms, using it at the earliest sign of a frontal headache or head cold.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing the center of the wrist instead of the thumb-side depression, or pressing so hard that you feel a sharp pinch toward the thumb. The target sensation is a deep, dull, spreading ache — ease off if it turns sharp or travels into the hand.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine LU7 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A full head-and-neck routine is more effective than pressing one point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 30–60 seconds each.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Frontal headache with stiff neck</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">GB20</a> → LU7 → <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4</a>, 30–60s each, repeat 1–2 times daily</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">First signs of a cold / scratchy throat</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">LU7 → <a href="/points/cv22-tiantu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">CV22</a> → LI4, gentle holds, plus warmth and fluids</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Stuffy nose with congestion</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">LU7 → <a href="/points/li20-yingxiang-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI20</a> → LI4, holding each 30–60s</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Desk-work neck tension (prevention)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">LU7 + GB20 at midday and again before bed; pair with posture breaks every 30–45 minutes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a fuller program, see the <a href="/body/head-acupressure-points" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">head acupressure points</a> body guide and our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-headache" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for headache</a> page.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">The point is often tender, especially if your neck is already tight. Most people feel the forehead pressure and neck stiffness ease within a few minutes of holding LU7 while turning the head, though symptoms can return as the day goes on. Do not expect a cure after one press.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With 1–2 sessions per day, tenderness at the point usually eases and the "headache with stiff neck" pattern becomes less frequent. This matches the pattern in studies of acupressure for tension-type headache, where benefits appear after several days of regular use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For recurring head and neck tension, keep LU7 in your routine and pair it with the points above. Acupressure is a complement, not a replacement — and a headache pattern that is new, worsening, or accompanied by neurological symptoms needs medical review rather than more pressing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure for headache and pain conditions, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. Manual stimulation of classic upper-limb points is commonly included in self-care protocols for head and neck tension, and several trials report meaningful reductions in headache intensity and frequency compared with no treatment, even though the overall methodological quality of the evidence is mixed. You can read a fuller assessment of what this evidence does and does not show on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work">does acupressure work?</a> page.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Point-specific research isolating LU7 on its own is scarce — the strongest findings come from broader studies of acupressure for tension-type headache and migraine. In traditional practice LU7 carries the specific classical indication of headache that connects to a stiff neck, and it is usually trained together with points on the neck and hands. Like most acupressure research, the results should be read as supportive and promising rather than definitive, and they do not replace medical treatment for persistent or severe symptoms.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["hsieh-2010", "song-2015"]} note="The reviews above evaluated acupressure for headache and for respiratory complaints, the two uses of LU7. Neither is a study of LU7 in isolation." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Avoid pressing if you have a recent wrist fracture, sprain, surgery, or a cast on that arm.</li>
      <li>Do not apply pressure over broken skin, a rash, a wart, or a bruise on the depression.</li>
      <li>Stop if pressure causes shooting pain, numbness, or tingling that radiates into the thumb or hand.</li>
      <li>Keep pressure light if you have carpal tunnel syndrome or nerve symptoms in the wrist, and stop if symptoms worsen.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>A sudden, severe "worst-ever" headache, especially after a blow to the head</li>
        <li>Headache with fever, a stiff neck, confusion, vision changes, or vomiting</li>
        <li>Cough with blood, high fever, or chest pain</li>
        <li>Weakness or numbness in the hand or arm</li>
        <li>Wrist or neck pain that has not improved after 2–3 weeks of regular acupressure</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How do I find LU7 on myself?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Cross the thumb and index finger of both hands as if shaking your own hands, and the tip of your index finger lands in the depression just above the bony bump (radial styloid process) on the thumb side of the wrist — roughly two finger widths above the wrist crease.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I press LU7?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Hold for 30–60 seconds per side, pressing toward the bone until you feel a deep, dull ache, and repeat on both arms 1–3 times a day. Consistency (daily) matters more than a single long session, and you can stop any time if you feel sharp pain, numbness, or tingling in the thumb or hand.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I press LU7 at work?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes. LU7 sits on the thumb side of the forearm and needs no special position, so a 30–60 second press at a desk is easy and discreet. Use it at the first sign of a frontal headache, while turning your head gently to loosen the neck.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can LU7 help with a cold or cough?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes. LU7 is classically used for a dry, irritating cough, a scratchy throat, and the stuffiness of a head cold. Press it for 30–60 seconds on each side while breathing evenly, and combine it with plenty of fluids. It is comfort care — not a treatment for an infection.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor instead?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">See a doctor for a sudden or severe headache, a headache with fever, a stiff neck, or vision changes, or any headache that follows a blow to the head. A cough with blood, a high fever, or chest pain also needs medical care, as does wrist or neck pain that has not improved after 2–3 weeks. See <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-headache" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for headache</a> for the full routine.</div>
    </details>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-10 text-center">
      <a href="/points" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-emerald-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-emerald-400">Back to All Points</a>
